{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/compound-bond.md; rg -n 'compound|convert|bond' scripts/check_accounting_dossiers.py; sed -n '1,130p' docs/design/research/standards-support.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/compound-bond.md; rg -n 'compound|convert|bond' scripts/check_accounting_dossiers.py; sed -n '1,130p' docs/design/research/standards-support.json\"","aggregated_output":"# Compound bond: issuer classification and settlement\n\nStatus: internally checked conditional illustration; actual instrument\nclassification is not established. MC17, MC19; gaps G4 and G7.\n\n## Facts and proposed answer\n\nThe issuer receives 100, owes two annual coupons of 5 and principal 100, and\nthe holder may convert that principal into ten ordinary shares. Currency is\nthe issuer's functional currency. Assume no fees, other options, contingencies\nor settlement alternatives; assume the share-conversion feature qualifies as\nequity. The market yield for otherwise comparable non-convertible debt is\nassumed to be 10%, not estimated from the example.\n\nUnder those conditions the proposed issuer split is liability\n`5/1.1 + 105/1.1² = 11050/121`, and residual equity `1050/121`.\nThe exact calculation keeps the components summing to 100. Using the stated\neffective-interest schedule, liability after coupons one and two is `1050/11`\nand 100. Interest expense totals `2260/121`, including discount accretion.\n\nIssue: debit cash 100; credit liability `11050/121` and conversion equity\n`1050/121`. Accrual debits interest expense and credits liability; each coupon\ndebits liability and credits cash 5. At maturity, redemption debits liability\nand credits cash 100. Conversion instead debits liability and credits issued\nshare equity 100; the original equity component remains in equity. The\n[checked journal report](../accounting-dossier-checks.json) preserves both\nsettlement paths. A 5% comparable yield gives liability 100 as an arithmetic\nboundary case; it is not evidence that a real conversion option has zero value.\n\n## Source and boundary\n\n[AASB 132](../../research/standards/aasb132.pdf), pp. 12–14 and 25–26:\nparagraph 22 describes fixed-number/fixed-amount settlement; 25–27 address\ncontingencies and settlement choices; 28–32 require the compound split and\nresidual; AG30 confines this treatment to the issuer, AG31 supplies comparable\nliability discounting, AG32 supplies no maturity-conversion gain/loss, and\nAG33–AG35 distinguish early repurchase and induced conversion. Paragraph 31\nrefers subsequent financial-liability measurement to AASB 9. This dossier's\naccretion calculation is a declared schedule; it does not fill the missing\nprimary measurement-source audit by implication.\n\nThe PDF's pp. 1 and 5 say the compilation applies to annual periods beginning\non/after 1 January 2023 and **before 1 July 2026**, incorporating amendments\nthrough 15 December 2022. Period start determines that condition; a calendar\n2026 annual period is inside it. The local source is an official Australian\nIAS-equivalent text with the comparison qualifications on p. 4, not proof of\nevery jurisdiction's currently adopted version.\n\n## Strongest counterargument and remaining internal work\n\nA variable share count, cash option, foreign-currency exercise amount, put,\nreset clause, contingent payment or related guarantee could defeat the assumed\nclassification. The arithmetic can remain perfectly balanced while the\nclassification is wrong. Do not apply this issuer example to the holder.\n\nFor an actual bond, first retain the complete contract and side agreements,\nprepare a clause-by-clause settlement table, establish reporting basis and\nperiod, acquire the applicable primary measurement authority, document the\ncomparable yield and test early settlement/modification where relevant.\nUS-GAAP and ASBE classification need their own dated sources. All are internal\npreparation tasks; do not pay for a reviewer to discover the missing clauses.\n\nA later review can ask whether named clauses X/Y change the proposed equity\nclassification under a cited paragraph, with both proposed treatments and\ntheir reconciled entries supplied. No actual clause requiring that judgment\nhas been supplied in the current synthetic example.\n49:    check('bond_issue_components', (liability, equity) == (Q(11050,121), Q(1050,121)))\n58:    redeemed, converted = book.copy(), book.copy()\n61:    post(converted, conversion_rows, 'conversion_at_maturity', liability=100, issued_share_equity=-100)\n62:    check('bond_settlement_paths', redeemed['liability'] == converted['liability'] == 0 and\n63:          converted['cash'] == 90 and converted['conversion_equity'] == -equity)\n65:    journals['bond_redemption'], journals['bond_conversion'] = redemption_rows, conversion_rows\n66:    values['bond'] = dict(issue_liability=str(liability), issue_equity=str(equity),\n{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"aasb102\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aasb.gov.au/admin/file/content105/c9/AASB102_07-15.pdf\",\n      \"path\": \"docs/design/research/standards/aasb102.pdf\",\n      \"sha256\": \"0d6fb4f59cc6daa7088f25e73542945d346f9c737ca85abf1a164a332bf93c06\",\n      \"access_date\": \"2026-10-02\",\n      \"inspected\": \"Comparison with IAS 2; paragraphs 23–27, 28–34\",\n      \"support_scope\": \"Official Australian adoption; for-profit relevant common text; not a claim about all later amendments\",\n      \"edition_applicability\": \"July 2015 issue; paragraph 40 applies to annual periods beginning on or after 2018-01-01, with the stated earlier-application provisions. Later amendments not checked in this pass.\",\n      \"edition_pages\": [\n        1,\n        10\n      ],\n      \"preparation_dossiers\": \"docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"aasb17\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aasb.gov.au/admin/file/content105/c9/AASB17_07-17_COMPdec22_01-23.pdf\",\n      \"path\": \"docs/design/research/standards/aasb17.pdf\",\n      \"sha256\": \"153e720cc1eaf7eea47d50463003391ea0433271694dd83a44c54a0e92deaec3\",\n      \"access_date\": \"2026-10-02\",\n      \"inspected\": \"Comparison with IFRS17; paragraphs 14–24, 32–38, 53–59, 60–70A, 78–82, B126–B127; reinspection of PAA, separate reinsurance recognition/eligibility, presentation and period applicability on 2 October 2026\",\n      \"support_scope\": \"Official compiled text; PAA service, gross/ceded and risk-adjustment examples only; no full compliance\",\n      \"edition_applicability\": \"Annual periods beginning on or after 2023-01-01 and before 2026-07-01; compiled amendments through 2022-12-15. Applicability uses period start, not the date of this review.\",\n      \"edition_pages\": [\n        1,\n        5\n      ],\n      \"preparation_dossiers\": \"docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"aasb132\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aasb.gov.au/admin/file/content105/c9/AASB132_08-15_COMPdec22_01-23.pdf\",\n      \"path\": \"docs/design/research/standards/aasb132.pdf\",\n      \"sha256\": \"5c8ce25447c6186ec49f751bc9e7ad48563caaff542b0e0287f3f7783a480501\",\n      \"access_date\": \"2026-10-02\",\n      \"inspected\": \"Comparison with IAS32; paragraphs 15–22, 28–32, AG30–AG35\",\n      \"support_scope\": \"Official compiled text; issuer compound split and maturity conversion; other classification conditions must be checked\",\n      \"edition_applicability\": \"Annual periods beginning on or after 2023-01-01 and before 2026-07-01; compiled amendments through 2022-12-15. Applicability uses period start, not the date of this review.\",\n      \"edition_pages\": [\n        1,\n        5\n      ],\n      \"preparation_dossiers\": \"docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"asbe-reproduction\",\n      \"url\": \"https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/企业会计准则第1号——存货\",\n      \"path\": \"docs/design/research/standards/asbe-reproduction.html\",\n      \"sha256\": \"ef50c4730a0c81f16ee419d63f6cade854dbc9a6a254938d05eeb9d920ebebd6\",\n      \"access_date\": \"2026-10-02\",\n      \"inspected\": \"Entire reproduced 2006 text, articles 1–22, particularly 14–19\",\n      \"support_scope\": \"Unofficial reproduction attributed to Ministry of Finance; official origin/version verification remains pending\",\n      \"preparation_dossiers\": \"docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"caterpillar2024\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.annualreports.com/HostedData/AnnualReportArchive/c/NYSE_CAT_2024.pdf\",\n      \"path\": \"docs/design/research/standards/caterpillar2024.pdf\",\n      \"sha256\": \"3a38651c724016bca792bef165fd42bb09d9b033d23718372a2599378768e925\",\n      \"access_date\": \"2026-10-02\",\n      \"inspected\": \"Note 1.C inventories and Note 8 inventories; note number corrected against the retained PDF on 2 October 2026\",\n      \"support_scope\": \"Company-authored report, mirror download; records disclosed US-GAAP LIFO policy, not full ASC330 authority\",\n      \"preparation_dossiers\": \"docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"blocked\": [\n    {\n      \"source\": \"FASB ASU2015-11 official PDF\",\n      \"reason\": \"403 in default and elevated requests; DART ASC330 primary text requires login; no bypass attempted\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source\": \"Ministry of Finance standards index\",\n      \"reason\": \"502 in default and elevated requests; unofficial text retained with verification pending\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '124,150p' docs/design/review/gap-audit-2026-10-02.md; sed -n '192,248p' docs/design/review/gap-audit-2026-10-02.md; rg -n '28|31|32|effective|2024|2025|2023|conversion|interest' docs/design/research/standards/aasb132.txt | head -65; tail -65 AGENTS.md; ls -l data/accounting-master/deliverables/P08 tests/test_accounting_p08.py scripts/corporate_financing_v1.py scripts/check_accounting_p08.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '124,150p' docs/design/review/gap-audit-2026-10-02.md; sed -n '192,248p' docs/design/review/gap-audit-2026-10-02.md; rg -n '28|31|32|effective|2024|2025|2023|conversion|interest' docs/design/research/standards/aasb132.txt | head -65; tail -65 AGENTS.md; ls -l data/accounting-master/deliverables/P08 tests/test_accounting_p08.py scripts/corporate_financing_v1.py scripts/check_accounting_p08.py\"","aggregated_output":"### G4. The first corporate accounting package is an inventory of duties\n\nEvidence: [initial corporate scope](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/chapters/08-modules.tex:41)\nand [report production](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/chapters/07-execution.tex:209).\n\nSales, inventory, payroll, fixed assets and working capital are named, but\nrecognition, measurement and transition rules are not supplied as a coherent\nreference package. There is no complete company example joining accruals,\nsettlements, cost of sales, depreciation, closing, cash-flow classification\nand changes in equity. Consolidation, foreign-currency translation and\nrestatement have sound stated requirements but lack equivalent numerical\nspecifications. The tests for these operations mostly describe desired\nproperties without complete inputs and expected outputs.\n\nRequired repair: provide one deliberately bounded corporate profile with an\nopening state, event schedules, journal templates and all statement mappings.\nCarry it across annual and interim periods, including a correction and one\nperimeter or currency case. Every supported operation needs its assumptions,\npreconditions, state changes and expected reports; explicit exclusions are\nacceptable. A balanced financing illustration does not specify this package.\n\n### G5. No complete filing-to-forecast reference case tests the central workflow\n\nEvidence: [proposed demonstrator](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/chapters/01-purpose.tex:128),\n[ingestion](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/chapters/07-execution.tex:6),\nand [example request](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/chapters/11-contracts.tex:234).\n\n### G7. Standards, banks and insurance are largely deferred requirements\n\nEvidence: [standards sources and limits](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/chapters/08-modules.tex:14),\n[banking](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/chapters/08-modules.tex:128),\nand [insurance](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/chapters/08-modules.tex:170).\n\nThe design explicitly relies on IFRS overview material rather than\nparagraph-level rule implementations. US GAAP and ASBE acquisition/review are\nfuture tasks. There is no matched economic-event example showing actual\ndifferences between the three reporting bases. Banking has an illustrative\none-horizon credit-loss calculation, but no specified allowance lifecycle or\nprudential-capital computation. Insurance names groups, cash flows, risk\nadjustments, service and finance effects without a complete cohort state and\nroll-forward. Hybrid instruments are described but not worked through competing\nsupported treatments.\n\nDeferring execution is reasonable. It means the document currently governs\nrequirements for those modules, not their implementation. Their claimed\ninfluence on the core representation also needs structural fixtures now, so\nthe types are tested before the later engines are built.\n\nRequired repair: define operation-level scope and primary authority for the\nfirst supported treatments. Add matched cross-standard examples and bank,\ninsurance and hybrid-instrument record/transition examples. Expand actuarial,\nregulatory and accounting literature alongside each method specification.\n\n### G8. Literature breadth exceeds the depth of the follow-up screening\n\nEvidence: [stated research scope](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/chapters/12-research.tex:76),\n[forward ledger](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/research/forward-snowball.json),\nand [ledger generator](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/scripts/build_design_research.py:182).\n\nThe 31 archived PDFs provide a broad starting point and important access gaps\nare honestly recorded. Nevertheless, 62 forward-search records contain 164\ncandidate records, of which 159 have the same generic disposition: outside\nthe core or a specialised extension, screened as metadata. The generator\nassigns that disposition by default and uses a few title-string exceptions.\nThese records do not establish 159 individually justified relevance decisions.\nFor example, a provenance-in-databases survey receives the blanket disposition\nalthough provenance is part of the proposed core. This does not establish that\nthe paper must be adopted; it does require a substantive exclusion rationale.\n\nThe four inaccessible sources are only one part of the gap. Accounting\nmeasurement, actuarial methods and some direct reconstruction/uncertainty\napproaches are expressly deferred. The current records therefore do not\nestablish a sufficiently deep survey for the entire requested implementation\nscope. They do not invalidate the individually supported claims already made.\n\nRequired repair: record actual screening decisions for relevant candidates;\nuse an unreviewed status until a decision exists. Inspect direct competitors,\nextensions, corrections and negative findings, expanding each implementation\nmethod's supporting literature. Do not treat the mere existence of six JSON\nledgers as evidence that the literature policy has been satisfied.\n\n### G9. Tool selection is a shortlist, without demonstrated adapter contracts\n\nEvidence: [software survey limits](/home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/docs/design/chapters/04-architecture.tex:83)\n1:Compiled AASB Standard                                                                          AASB 132\n7:This compiled Standard applies to annual periods beginning on or after 1 January 2023 but before 1 July 2026.\n8:Earlier application is permitted for annual periods beginning after 24 July 2014 but before 1 January 2023. It\n10:Prepared on 6 April 2023 by the staff of the Australian Accounting Standards Board.\n12:Compilation date: 31 December 2022\n17:                                  Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n42:© Commonwealth of Australia 2023\n57:AASB 132-compiled                                           2                                          COPYRIGHT\n58:                                   Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n61:COMPARISON WITH IAS 32\n64:AASB 132 FINANCIAL INSTRUMENTS: PRESENTATION\n81:Compound financial instruments                                                                                     28\n93:DELETED IAS 32 TEXT\n98:Basis for Conclusions on IAS 32\n102:Australian Accounting Standard AASB 132 Financial Instruments: Presentation (as amended) is set out in paragraphs\n104:main principles. AASB 132 is to be read in the context of other Australian Accounting Standards, including\n112:AASB 132-compiled                                          3                                            CONTENTS\n113:                                  Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n114:\fComparison with IAS 32\n115:AASB 132 Financial Instruments: Presentation as amended incorporates IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation\n117:(which are not included in IAS 32) are identified with the prefix “Aus”. Paragraphs that apply only to not-for-profit\n122:For-profit entities complying with AASB 132 also comply with IAS 32.\n123:Not-for-profit entities’ compliance with IAS 32 will depend on whether any “Aus” paragraphs that specifically apply\n125:IAS 32.\n136:AASB 132-compiled                                          4                                        COMPARISON\n137:                                  Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n138:\fAccounting Standard AASB 132\n139:The Australian Accounting Standards Board made Accounting Standard AASB 132 Financial Instruments:\n141:This compiled version of AASB 132 applies to annual periods beginning on or after 1 January 2023 but before 1 July\n146:Accounting Standard AASB 132\n162:         instruments; the classification of related interest, dividends, losses and gains; and the circumstances in\n171:         (a)         those interests in subsidiaries, associates or joint ventures that are accounted for in\n173:                     Statements or AASB 128 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. However, in some\n174:                     cases, AASB 10, AASB 127 or AASB 128 require or permit an entity to account for an\n175:                     interest in a subsidiary, associate or joint venture using AASB 9; in those cases, entities\n177:                     derivatives linked to interests in subsidiaries, associates or joint ventures.\n191:AASB 132-compiled                                               5                                            STANDARD\n192:                                     Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n229:                     instruments is exempt from applying to these features paragraphs 15–32 and AG25–AG35\n252:AASB 132-compiled                                             6                                            STANDARD\n253:                                     Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n309:AASB 132-compiled                                          7                                               STANDARD\n310:                                  Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n324:        An equity instrument is any contract that evidences a residual interest in the assets of an entity after\n338:        •         effective interest method\n343:        •         hedge effectiveness\n366:AASB 132-compiled                                           8                                         STANDARD\n367:                                   Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n426:AASB 132-compiled                                          9                                                STANDARD\n427:                                  Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n483:AASB 132-compiled                                          10                                               STANDARD\n484:                                  Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n536:                  members with a right to redeem their interests in the issuer at any time for cash, which results in\n537:                  the unitholders’ or members’ interests being classified as financial liabilities, except for those\n546:AASB 132-compiled                                          11                                              STANDARD\n547:                                  Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n548:\f                        disclosure to show that total members’ interests comprise items such as reserves that meet the\n583:            interest rate, a commodity price or a financial instrument price). Two examples are (a) a contract to deliver\n589:            evidence a residual interest in the entity’s assets after deducting all of its liabilities.\n594:            equity instrument. Changes in the fair value of a contract arising from variations in market interest rates that\n609:AASB 132-compiled                                                   12                                            STANDARD\n610:                                         Authorised Version F2023C00406 registered 04/05/2023\n639:        interest rate or taxation requirements, or the issuer’s future revenues, net income or debt-to-equity ratio. The\n664:28      The issuer of a non-derivative financial instrument shall evaluate the terms of the financial\n670:AASB 132-compiled                                          13                                                STANDARD\n# AccDSL project instructions\n\n## Mission entry point\n\nBefore substantive work in this project, read:\n\n1. [Mission control](MISSION_CONTROL.md).\n2. [Requirements and acceptance evidence](docs/plans/accounting-mission-requirements.md).\n3. [Current checkpoint](docs/plans/accounting-product-design-checkpoint.md).\n4. The gap records relevant to the task:\n   [initial audit](docs/design/review/gap-audit-2026-10-02.md) and\n   [expanded mission audit](docs/design/review/mission-gap-audit-2026-10-02.md).\n5. For the authorised master-program work, read the\n   [master checkpoint](docs/plans/accounting-master-checkpoint.md), executable\n   phase catalog and current `data/accounting-master/next-plan.md`. The older\n   corpus checkpoint remains historical evidence; current phase execution is\n   recorded by the master without rewriting frozen release hashes.\n\nThe mission preserves the user's goals across sessions. Current user directions\nand higher-priority instructions still control. Local documents must not be\nused to request fresh permission for work already authorised by the user.\n\n## Working rules\n\n- Identify the MC requirements addressed by each nontrivial plan or work unit.\n  Audit the plan before implementation, experiments or long diagnostics.\n- Preserve the full goals while staging execution. A small financing demo,\n  clean PDF build or six populated research ledgers cannot close requirements\n  for whole-company representation, research quality or implementation detail.\n- Distinguish direct user requirements, policy requirements and derived\n  engineering choices. Do not silently convert a convenient library, model\n  assumption or stage boundary into an immutable product requirement.\n- Keep source evidence, interpretations, assumptions, joint history families\n  and scenario-specific results separate and traceable. Preserve unknowns;\n  do not invent facts, probabilities, accounting compliance or completeness.\n- Close gaps with the specified evidence, and update the requirements register\n  and checkpoint after meaningful changes. Add newly discovered gaps rather\n  than treating the current list as exhaustive.\n- Follow the applicable policies under\n  `/home/chakwong/python/claudecodex/policies`. Preserve unrelated work and the\n  original paper collection. The existing `.git` marker is not a usable\n  repository; do not initialise or commit merely to create provenance.\n- Keep operational mission/checklist material out of the reader-facing\n  scholarly narrative unless it serves a real implementation-reference purpose.\n- Apply MC35, the user's internal-preparation-before-paid-review requirement.\n  Finish feasible source research, facts, calculations, software checks and\n  document repairs before referring a specific question to a professional.\n  Supply a proposed answer, exact sources, competing interpretation and checked\n  consequences. Keep unfinished internal work on the internal queue; “human\n  review pending” is an acceptance status, not a reason to stop that work.\n  Ordinary reader feedback need not be purchased from an accounting firm.\n  No professional engagement, outreach or fees are authorised by this policy.\n  See `docs/design/review/internal-before-expert.md` and the current checkpoint.\n\nThe current manuscript is specification-incomplete. Read the checkpoint and\nrequirements before resuming from an older note that says only final review\nremains. This file authorises no autonomous background campaign or external\npublication; it directs work undertaken under the user's active instructions.\nls: cannot access 'data/accounting-master/deliverables/P08': No such file or directory\nls: cannot access 'tests/test_accounting_p08.py': No such file or directory\nls: cannot access 'scripts/corporate_financing_v1.py': No such file or directory\nls: cannot access 'scripts/check_accounting_p08.py': No such file or directory\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,95p' MISSION_CONTROL.md; sed -n '1,100p' docs/plans/accounting-master-checkpoint.md; sed -n '1,60p' docs/plans/accounting-product-design-checkpoint.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,95p' MISSION_CONTROL.md; sed -n '1,100p' docs/plans/accounting-master-checkpoint.md; sed -n '1,60p' docs/plans/accounting-product-design-checkpoint.md\"","aggregated_output":"# AccDSL mission control\n\nEstablished 2 October 2026 (Asia/Hong_Kong) at the user's request.\nStatus: active mission; Version 1.2 is a repaired design candidate with checked\nreference cases. Full product, domain and human acceptance remain pending.\n\n## Purpose\n\nDesign a system that represents a company's historical financial disclosures,\npreserves economically different histories and interpretations compatible with\nthe evidence, and carries those alternatives into scenario-dependent future\njournals and financial statements. Preserve their separate conditional outcome\ndistributions or feasible sets, including uncertainty that cannot be assigned\ndefensible probabilities.\n\nThe immediate deliverable is a detailed, readable LaTeX product and method\ndesign, with compiled PDF, supporting literature and software investigation,\nprecise implementation contracts and acceptance examples. The eventual product\nis the working representation and simulation engine. A document build, a small\nfinancing example and an implemented prototype are different achievements.\n\n## Authority and origin\n\nThis mission records the user's existing directions; it does not replace new\nuser instructions or higher-priority instructions. The three original requests\nare preserved in saved session `01a0f8e0-0c0b-7332-8f7e-d6b93ce32761`, at JSONL\nrecords 10, 115 and 145. They establish whole-company history, heterogeneous\naccounting, preservation of alternative journals/outcomes, a broad literature\nand software survey, and an implementation-governing LaTeX document. The current\nrequest establishes this durable mission and a requirements-based gap audit.\n\nThe user's subsequent direction establishes MC35: complete feasible internal\npreparation before paying for professional review. The user supplied a planning\nrate of US$500–1,200/hour. This work order is binding; no estimated savings or\nprofessional engagement is inferred from it.\n\nThe [requirements register](docs/plans/accounting-mission-requirements.md)\ndistinguishes direct user requirements from policy obligations and engineering\nrequirements derived from those goals. Python, storage engines, parser design,\nsolver libraries and a particular first-company example remain replaceable\nimplementation proposals. Designing a DSL is part of the work; claiming it is\nbetter than all alternatives is not a predetermined conclusion.\n\n## Main goals\n\n| Goal | Required outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| M1 — Represent the historical record | Preserve annual, quarterly and semi-annual statements, notes, policies and relevant referenced disclosures, with their source, context, temporal meaning and unresolved content. |\n| M2 — Preserve alternative explanations | Represent joint families of admissible histories, journal movements, contract states and interpretations; distinguish absent facts, accounting choices, estimates and future randomness. |\n| M3 — Simulate financial evolution | Apply explicit economic scenarios and behaviour to economic state, generate admissible journal entries, and produce coherent future statements and conditional results. |\n| M4 — Handle accounting and sector diversity | Accommodate IFRS, US GAAP and China GAAP/ASBE, industry and company differences, group boundaries, banks, insurers, liabilities, capital, bonds and equity. |\n| M5 — Specify implementable methods and language | Define reconstruction, accounting, inference and execution procedures, types, IR, DSL, interfaces, failure behaviour and persistence precisely enough for compatible implementations. |\n| M6 — Ground choices in research and reusable tools | Inspect primary literature beyond the existing collection and official software, archive relevant sources, compare credible alternatives and document justified inclusion/exclusion and reuse decisions. |\n| M7 — Deliver the governing design | Produce one rigorous, understandable LaTeX/PDF argument with complete worked cases, explicit scope, source support, method details and an implementation sequence. |\n| M8 — Establish evidence and maintain direction | Define conformance and release evidence, preserve reproducibility, keep engineering/accounting/scientific conclusions separate, and maintain requirements and open decisions as work proceeds. |\n\n## Requirements that must survive every implementation choice\n\n1. Source preservation is broader than numerical extraction. Account for all\n   source material within the declared disclosure package, including notes and\n   cross-references. Content that cannot yet be formalised stays visible with\n   a reason; it is not silently excluded from a completeness claim.\n2. Preserve reported assertions, derived consequences and modelling assumptions\n   separately. A missing value is not zero; a reported number is not automatic\n   proof of the underlying economic state. Source conflicts remain inspectable.\n3. Carry entity, group perimeter, concept, dimensions, units, currency, basis,\n   precision and time through every calculation. Label mapping does not itself\n   convert between accounting standards. Avoid double counting overlapping\n   classifications.\n4. Preserve original and revised information. Economic time, reporting periods,\n   availability/publication and system ingestion/version time answer different\n   questions. Historical forecasts cannot use later evidence inadvertently.\n5. Keep alternatives jointly consistent across statements, notes, dates,\n   contracts and accounts. Include economic state beyond journals: identical\n   historical postings can conceal different future exposures.\n6. Every reconstructed family has an explicit model scope and completeness\n   status. A selected witness, bounded search or convenient aggregate cannot be\n   presented as all real histories. Scope exclusions remain mission limitations.\n7. Preserve conditional results by explanation and scenario. A set or interval\n   does not become a probability distribution without a declared measure. No\n   weights from representation counts, arbitrary equality or LLM confidence.\n   Keep dependence between statement amounts and through time.\n8. Generate events from explicit contract and behavioural assumptions, then\n   apply recognition/measurement and posting rules. Balanced journals are\n   necessary but do not prove accounting treatment or economic correctness.\n9. Treat unsupported calculations, unresolved branches, numerical failure and\n   economically meaningful default as distinct outcomes. Do not silently drop\n   failed branches, manufacture financing or report sampled extrema as exact\n   bounds.\n10. Compare a DSL with credible alternatives on the same tasks, especially the\n    typed-library/shared-IR baseline. Reuse software where its inspected\n    capabilities fit, with explicit adapter contracts and limits.\n11. Support imported technical claims with inspected primary sections and local\n    copies. Preserve relevant papers under `docs/papers` using\n    `Title First-author-surname (year).pdf`; preserve the original collection.\n# Accounting master checkpoint\n\nUpdated 3 October 2026, Hong Kong, during run `20261002T233421-cea9e631`.\nThe user's create/review/execute instruction and original standing grant remain\nactive. The catalog preserves all MC01–MC35 and G1–G18, with no missing IDs.\n\n## Active run and next action\n\nTool session `78463` is running. P00–P04 are accepted internally; P05 is\nplanning. Do not launch another master or edit controller files while it is\nactive. Poll the session and consult current state before resuming. This is\na dated checkpoint; saved state and events record subsequent changes.\n\n```text\npython3 /home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/scripts/run_accounting_master.py status\n```\n\nAfter this invocation exits, resume with:\n\n```text\npython3 /home/chakwong/python/AccDSL/scripts/run_accounting_master.py run\n```\n\nThe current invocation has the configured 7,200-second wall limit, sequential\nworkers and bounded repair attempts. A budget boundary retains partial work\nand the next plan; it does not complete the mission.\n\n- [State](../../data/accounting-master/state.json)\n- [Next plan](../../data/accounting-master/next-plan.md)\n- [Events](../../data/accounting-master/events.jsonl)\n- [Active manifest](../../data/accounting-master/runs/20261002T233421-cea9e631/manifest.json)\n- [Executable plan](accounting-master-program.md)\n- [Preserved previous checkpoint](../../data/accounting-master/checkpoints/before-p04-acceptance-20261003.md)\n\n## Current evidence\n\nThe prior run `20261002T221346-d54a6552` ended at 23:30:11 UTC on 2 October\nwith `wall_limit`, exit code 2. P03 was accepted. P04's repair/checks passed,\nbut its result reviewer had only about two seconds before the run limit and\nproduced no verdict. That timeout was not promoted to acceptance.\n\nThe resumed run began at 23:34:21 UTC. All 81 controller tests passed freshly,\nand the unchanged controller's finding adjudication was reused. The continuation\naudit retained the accepted 192-report baseline, original criteria, independent\nnumerical comparators, bounded workers and distinct evidence classes. Source,\ncontroller and accepted-evidence hashes matched; state/plan consistency passed.\nNo controller, dependency, permission or scientific-default change was required.\n\n| Phase | Observed state | Scope and next work |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| P00/P01 | Accepted internally | Frozen baseline and 192 source-context records; semantics, availability and benchmark annotations remain open. |\n| P02 | Accepted internally | Selected Apple note dispositions and reconciliations; full company and agreement semantics remain open. |\n| P03 | Accepted internally | Core 0 source/typed-input lowering; 11 tests, 101 obligations. General language, mappings and uncertainty remain open. |\n| P04 | Accepted internally at 23:58:10 UTC | Executed reconstruction/provenance repair; 21 P04 tests/obligations and 11 P03 regressions passed; clean read-only AGREE result review. |\n| P05 | Running: planning | Generate journals/statements from economic events; existing Northstar postings are the independent comparator, not event input. |\n| P08 | Needs repair, independently eligible | Prior planning interruption is resumable. |\n| P06/P07/P09–P14 | Pending | Dependencies control scheduling. |\n\nP04 now records current approval/execution provenance and preserves historical\nreferences. Added checks reject malformed/mismatched provenance and ensure honest\nfailure reports. All 394 recorded hashes and 384 protected inputs matched. The\nreviewer independently reran 17 numerical and nine P03 tests. Four P04 and two\nP03 checker tests relied on matching supervisor evidence and inspected code\nbecause their writes were unavailable in the review sandbox. The reviewer\nreturned AGREE, exited zero without timeout, and took 238.855 seconds.\n\nThe master immediately refreshed the plan and started P05. Generation 57 matched\nsaved state when inspected; all accepted P00–P04 evidence hashes matched. Earlier\nworker text saying independent acceptance is pending is superseded by the clean\nP04 review and accepted state. Accounting, forecast and human acceptance remain\nunresolved.\n\n- [P04 repair response](../../data/accounting-master/runs/20261002T233421-cea9e631/P04/execution-0/implement-0.response.json)\n- [P04 supervisor checks](../../data/accounting-master/runs/20261002T233421-cea9e631/P04/execution-0/checks.json)\n- [P04 result review](../../data/accounting-master/runs/20261002T233421-cea9e631/P04/execution-0/review_result-0.response.json)\n- [P04 review process](../../data/accounting-master/runs/20261002T233421-cea9e631/P04/execution-0/review_result-0.process.json)\n- [P04 conformance](../../data/accounting-master/deliverables/P04/conformance.json)\n\n## Approval and continuation\n\nThe installed `/home/chakwong/.codex/rules/accdsl-master.rules` again returned\n`allow` for the exact absolute run command; an unrelated Python script did not\nmatch. This invocation resumed without new approval. Use the same entrypoint\ndirectly, without wrappers, environment prefixes or changing relative paths.\nWorkers retain workspace-write/read-only sandboxes and `never` nested approvals.\nManaged policy remains authoritative outside the existing grant.\n\nContinue eligible phases through the runner. At exit inspect its manifest,\nstate/plan consistency, phase evidence and recorded child cleanup before resume.\nRetain partial work and specific repair findings. Use `--retry-blocked` only\nafter a known external access/input change. The current local policy permits\ncontinued internal work while human feedback is pending.\n\nNo paid data, professional engagement, outreach, accounts, deployment, publication,\nglobal/conda installation, Git initialization or parallel/recursive workers are\nauthorised. Protected release sources and historical evidence remain unchanged.\nThe unusable Git marker is preserved; content hashes provide provenance.\nAll G1–G18 remain open and `mission_complete` remains false. Bounded phase\nacceptance does not establish arbitrary-history completeness, full accounting\nvalidity, empirical forecast validity or human acceptance.\n# Active checkpoint\n\nUpdated: 2 October 2026 (Asia/Hong_Kong), after multi-year source-corpus\nacceptance following the expanded-audit repair. The user directed us to do all\nfeasible internal work before costly professional review. MC35 preserves that work order.\nThe wider mission remains active; human review is not the next automatic step.\n\n## Accepted source corpus and next execution\n\nThe user's corpus-first work unit is complete at the source-acquisition layer:\n**192 of 192 target reports, 2018–2025, across nine issuers and three evidenced\naccounting families. All nine have eight consecutive complete years.**\n`target-panel-v2.json` is active and is now the collector's explicit default.\nHenkel replaces Unilever and Munich Re replaces Allianz under the reviewed\nsource-access repair; frozen v1 coverage and all original files remain under\n`data/financial-report-benchmark/snapshots/`.\n\nResume from the [corpus result](accounting-benchmark-corpus-results.md),\n`data/financial-report-benchmark/index.html`, `coverage.json` and\n`integrity-manifest.json`. QA 6 revalidated all 244 retained original versions;\nthe independent hash/provenance/grid/PDF checks pass, and 20 regression tests\npass. The old 12-, 157- and 159-report counts are historical. There are 738\nattempt/revalidation records, not 738 benchmark observations.\n\nThe collector checkpoints each attempt and supports `--revalidate-retained`,\n`--resolved-only`, sequential `--index-plan` capture and bounded host failures.\nOfficial HSBC and Midea archives, SAIC year pages, Ping An's published report\nfeed, SEC submissions and issuer-hosted US filing feeds have been resolved.\nDirect SEC archive downloads returned 403; issuer-hosted copies are used with\nthe regulator identity and filing dates retained. Run one acquisition writer\nat a time. The optional `--http-client curl` bounds total request time;\n`--source-host` can select a resolved alternate without retrying known failures.\nPing An's last ASBE slot was filled by an A-share report attachment on a public\nmirror; the distinct IFRS copy remains excluded from that slot. Do not reset\nthe ledger, initialise Git or promote source checks into accounting evidence.\n\nThe next internal work is task-specific literal/context/span annotation and\nCore 0 semantic/compiler implementation using the preserved source set and\nexisting fixtures. `benchmark-protocol-v1.json` and\n`annotation-record.schema.json` define the proposed tasks and exchange format;\nissuer-family roles are provisional, with no model score or gold labels.\nResolve incorporated documents, revisions and publication timing before\nhistorical forecasting. Publication dates are unverified for 136 of 192\naccepted reports; 56 have filing-date metadata. No paid review is triggered.\n\n## Current deliverable\n\n`docs/design/accounting-product-design.pdf` and its LaTeX sources are Version\n1.2, a 79-page design candidate with 11 chapters and two appendices. It contains\ncomplete Northstar annual/interim/revision/forecast cases, an Apple filing\ninventory and conditional financing stress, historical construction and solver\nlimits, corporate operations, sector examples, Core 0 contracts,\nreview/replay/adapter definitions and corpus/release protocols.\n\nThe 8 mission goals and MC01–MC34 retain their requirement and acceptance\nwording. MC35 adds the new cost/preparation requirement. Assessments identify\nevidence and residual obligations. A bounded debt semantic validator, frozen\nregistry and three accounting source dossiers supplement the design. Version\n1.2 corrects the Caterpillar note anchor and states separate reinsurance PAA\neligibility/recognition/timing; it preserves the prior calculations.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
